                <description>
                category  : See &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Ferahevler, Nuri Paşa Cd. No:107, Sarıyer  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : https://www.atamanmuseum.com/tr/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  :  &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2369">
                    <coordinates>29.047759190721585,41.128078506849675,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2372">
                <name>Emirgan Park </name>
                <description>
                category  : See &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : Situated on the gentle hill overlooking the neighbourhood of Emirgan and the Bosphorus, Emirgan Park was for long the only place in the city where Istanbulites can admire the beauty of tulips. Having lost that distinction since the first decade of the 2000s as tulips are now everywhere, this is still a beautiful park with artificial ponds, small waterfalls, and impressive views of the Bosphorus. There are also cafes with open-air sections housed in pleasant former imperial hunting manors. Squirrels are there, too, in the middle of this metropolis of more than 15 million people, though you may have to look a bit deeper (or a bit upper on the branches!) to spot them. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2371">
                    <coordinates>29.0525,41.108333,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2374">
                <name>Yusuf Ziya Mansion </name>
                <description>
                category  : See &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Rumeli Hisarı, Balta Limanı Hisar Cd. No:5, Sarıyer  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :  https://www.borusancontemporary.com/ &lt;br&gt;
                description  :  It houses the headquarters of Borusan Holding and the Borusan Contemporary art museum. The popular nickname of the mansion, ''Perili Köşk'' (meaning the &quot;Haunted Mansion&quot;) refers to its construction remaining partially unfinished for a long period (its second and third floors remained empty for decades.) &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2373">
                    <coordinates>29.056555050745292,41.08912990773445,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
