            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2572">
                <name>The Jewish Museum of Turkey </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around Galata Tower_Jewish heritage &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Bereketzade Mh, Büyük Hendek Cd 39, Beyoğlu  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.muze500.com/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A former synagogue, the museum's exhibit details how the cultures mixed with and influenced each other in the past 500+ years since the Spanish expulsion. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2571">
                    <coordinates>28.9727283,41.0267866,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2574">
                <name>Camondo Stairs </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around Galata Tower_Jewish heritage &lt;br&gt;
                address  :   &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : An attractive stairway mixing the Neo-Baroque and early Art Nouveau styles, climbing up from Bankalar St towards the Galata Tower. It was built in the 1870s by Abraham Salomon Camondo, who belonged to a prominent Ottoman-Venetian Jewish family of financiers and philanthropists. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2573">
                    <coordinates>28.97378,41.02397,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2576">
                <name>Schneidertempel </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Around Galata Tower_Jewish heritage &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Bereketzade, Bankalar Cad./felek Sok. No:1  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.schneidertempel.org/ &lt;br&gt;
                description  : This former synagogue of Ashkenazi Jews (many of German or French origin) now hosts an art gallery downstairs. Upstairs, there is an exhibition about the arrival and the lives of Ashkenazi Jews in Istanbul, with many original exhibits, including a letter from Albert Einstein. All explanations are in both Turkish and English. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2575">
                    <coordinates>28.974002326991922,41.02411637715728,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2578">
                <name>Pera Museum </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Along İstiklal Caddesi &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Meşrutiyet Cd 65  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://en.peramuzesi.org.tr/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A private museum with a large painting collection and archaeological collections of measurement units and tools used in Asia Minor since antiquity and faiences of [[Kütahya]]. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2577">
                    <coordinates>28.975139,41.031833,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2580">
                <name>French Institute </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Along İstiklal Caddesi &lt;br&gt;
                address  : İstiklal Cd 4  &lt;br&gt;
                url  : http://www.ifturquie.org/  &lt;br&gt;
                description  : They have good art exhibits for free and sometimes have French films in the cinema. &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2579">
                    <coordinates>28.9839866,41.0362358,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
            <Placemark id="2582">
                <name>Hagia Triada Greek Orthodox Church </name>
                <description>
                category  : See_Along İstiklal Caddesi &lt;br&gt;
                address  : Taksim Square  &lt;br&gt;
                url  :   &lt;br&gt;
                description  : A charming domed church built in 1880 at the side of Taksim Square, now uneasily contesting with Taksim Mosque for being the main landmark of the southern edge of the square.&lt;!----&gt;[[Image:Istanbul asv2021-11 img71 StAnthony of Padua Church.jpg &lt;br&gt;
                </description>
                <styleUrl>#110</styleUrl>
                <Point id="2581">
                    <coordinates>28.9842,41.0355,0.0</coordinates>
                </Point>
            </Placemark>
